=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Two Major Types


@left

- Agarose: physical gel setting; easy casting and handling
- Polyacrylamide (PAGE): acrylamide + bis-acrylamide
- Safety: uncured acrylamide is a potent neurotoxin

@top-right


![Instructor figure related to protein gels and western blotting](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_006.png)


@bottom-right


![Acrylamide and bis-acrylamide chemical structures](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_008.png)
=== layout: two-panels


# Agarose Gel


@left


![Instructor figure related to protein gels and western blotting](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_003.png)

![Instructor figure related to protein gels and western blotting](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_004.png)


@right


- Agarose is added to buffer solution (TAE)
- Place in microwave to boil
- Then pour hot solution into gel cassette
- Commonly used for nucleic acid NOT protein
=== layout: two-panels


# Agarose Polymer


@left


- Agar melts at 85  deg C (185  deg F) and solidifies around 32-40  deg C (90-104  deg F). This property lends a suitable balance between easy melting and good gel stability at relatively high temperatures.
- Gel density is controlled by concentration of agarose


@right


![Instructor figure related to protein gels and western blotting](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_006.png)
=== layout: two-panels


