# 2. Metabolomics


@top-left


![Instructor figure related to metabolite profile](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_002.jpg)


@bottom-left


![Instructor figure related to metabolite profile](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_041.png)


@right


- Metabolite profile
- Chemical fingerprints
- Cell physiology
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: Metabolomics


@left


- What does metabolomics measure, and how is it different from genomics and proteomics?
- How do mass spectrometry and NMR reveal small molecules in a sample?
- How can metabolomics complement protein measurements in biotechnology or systems biology?


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to metabolite profile](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_002.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to metabolite profile](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_041.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Metabolomics


@left


- Metabolomics measures small molecules associated with metabolism, such as sugars, amino acids, and lipids.
- A metabolome is a condition-sensitive snapshot shaped by genes, proteins, environment, and recent activity.
- Mass spectrometry and NMR detect different chemical properties; chromatography often separates mixtures before MS.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to metabolite profile](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_041.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to metabolite profile](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_001.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Metabolomics - Use with other data


@left


- Proteomics measures proteins; metabolomics measures small molecules produced, consumed, or modified by cellular activity.
- Combining molecular layers can connect a changed pathway to both its enzymes and its chemical products.
- Sample handling and platform coverage matter: no single method measures every metabolite equally well.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to metabolite profile](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_001.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to metabolite profile](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_002.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Metabolomics - What metabolomics measures


@left


- Metabolites include sugars, amino acids, organic acids, lipids, and other small molecules in a sample.
- The metabolome changes with nutrient supply, temperature, stress, treatment, and recent cell activity.
- Mass spectrometry and nuclear magnetic resonance measure different chemical properties and provide complementary coverage.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to metabolite profile](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_002.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to metabolite profile](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_041.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Metabolomics - Measure a changing metabolome


@left


- Quench metabolism quickly at collection so the measured profile reflects the sampled condition.
- Chromatography can separate compounds before mass spectrometry; NMR can identify compounds from their magnetic environments.
- Use internal standards, pooled quality-control samples, blanks, and replicates to track analytical variation.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to metabolite profile](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_041.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to metabolite profile](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_001.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Metabolomics - Connect metabolites to proteins


@left


- A metabolite change can suggest a pathway is more active, less active, or limited by a substrate.
- Pairing metabolite profiles with protein abundance and enzyme activity helps distinguish pathway capacity from output.
- Association suggests a relationship; perturbation experiments test whether a molecule causes the change.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to metabolite profile](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_001.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to metabolite profile](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_002.jpg)
=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


# 3. Protein Gels


@top-left


![Instructor figure related to gel matrix](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_027.png)


@bottom-left


![Instructor figure related to gel matrix](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_028.png)


@right


- Gel matrix
- Protein charge
- Band visualization
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: Protein Gels


@left


- How does polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis separate proteins, and how does it differ from DNA agarose gels?
- What do SDS, reducing agents, stacking gels, and gradient gels contribute?
- How are protein bands stained and interpreted?


@top-right


![Student-created diagram of SDS and disulfide-reducing sample preparation.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_sds-and-reducing-preparation.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Student-created comparison of stacking and gradient gels.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_stacking-and-gradient-gels.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Gels


@left


- In SDS-PAGE, SDS unfolds many proteins and gives them a similar charge-to-mass ratio; smaller polypeptides usually migrate farther.
- DTT or beta-mercaptoethanol reduces disulfide bonds; a stacking gel concentrates samples before the resolving gel.
- Polyacrylamide pore size suits protein separation; gel percentage or a gradient changes the size range resolved.


@top-right


![Student-created comparison of stacking and gradient gels.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_stacking-and-gradient-gels.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Student-created sequence for staining, destaining, and visualizing protein bands.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_coomassie-staining.jpg)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: Protein Gels - Visualizing bands


@left


- Coomassie dye binds proteins and produces visible bands after excess stain is washed away.
- Silver stains and fluorescent stains can detect lower amounts, with different sensitivity and workflow tradeoffs.
- Band position estimates apparent molecular mass; band intensity is only a rough abundance measure without calibration.


@right


![Protein gel electrophoresis apparatus from the instructor ODP](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_003.png)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: Protein Gels - Build the protein gel


@left


- Polyacrylamide forms when acrylamide monomers cross-link with bis-acrylamide; their ratio tunes pore size.
- Acrylamide monomer is hazardous before polymerization and requires appropriate protective procedures.
- Polyacrylamide provides smaller, tunable pores for proteins than standard agarose gels.


@right


![Student-created illustration of adjustable polyacrylamide gel pores.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_polyacrylamide-pores.jpg)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: Protein Gels - Prepare proteins for SDS-PAGE


@left


- SDS unfolds proteins and coats them with a broadly similar negative charge per unit length.
- DTT or beta-mercaptoethanol reduces disulfide bonds so linked chains can separate.
- Heating helps denature many samples; adjust conditions for proteins that aggregate or remain unusually stable.


@right


![Student-created diagram of SDS and disulfide-reducing sample preparation.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_sds-and-reducing-preparation.jpg)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: Protein Gels - Choose and stain a gel


@left


- A stacking gel concentrates proteins into a narrow starting zone before they enter the resolving gel.
- A gradient gel spans a wider mass range; a uniform gel gives resolution across a narrower range.
- Coomassie reveals protein bands in visible light; silver and fluorescent stains can detect lower amounts.


@right


![Student-created comparison of stacking and gradient gels.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_stacking-and-gradient-gels.jpg)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: Protein Gels - Staining and reading bands


@left


- Coomassie dye binds proteins and reveals bands in visible light after excess stain is washed away.
- Stain and destain conditions affect background, sensitivity, and compatibility with later analysis.
- Compare band position with markers; use calibration and controls before interpreting intensity quantitatively.


@right


![Student-created sequence for staining, destaining, and visualizing protein bands.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_coomassie-staining.jpg)
=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


# 4. Western Blotting


@top-left


![Instructor figure related to antibody target](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_031.jpg)


@bottom-left


![Instructor figure related to antibody target](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_032.png)


@right


- Antibody target
- Protein transfer
- Signal detection
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: Western Blotting


@left


- How does a Western blot detect one protein among the bands in a sample?
- How does it compare with Southern blotting and with an ELISA?
- Which controls help distinguish a real target signal from a failed or nonspecific result?


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to antibody target](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_031.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to antibody target](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_032.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Western Blotting


@left


- Proteins are separated by electrophoresis, transferred to a membrane, and probed with target-specific antibodies.
- A labeled secondary antibody or directly labeled primary antibody generates a detectable signal at the target band.
- A loading control, positive control, negative control, and appropriate size marker test different parts of the workflow.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to antibody target](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_032.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to antibody target](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_020.jpg)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: Western Blotting - Reading the result


@left


- Southern blots detect DNA; Western blots detect proteins; both transfer separated molecules to a membrane for probe-based detection.
- Western blots support target identity and approximate size, but signal intensity is not automatically an absolute protein concentration.
- Common failure points include poor transfer, unsuitable antibodies, high background, and signal saturation.


@right


![Example Western blot with protein bands across sample lanes](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_020.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Western Blotting - Western blot workflow


@left


- Separate proteins by electrophoresis, transfer them to a membrane, and block unoccupied binding sites.
- An antibody recognizes the target; a labeled primary or secondary antibody produces a measurable signal.
- Band position estimates apparent size, while band intensity requires careful controls for quantification.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to antibody target](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_029.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to antibody target](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_030.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Western Blotting - Controls answer different questions


@left


- A molecular-mass marker checks migration; a loading control helps compare sample input.
- A positive control tests target detection; a negative control helps reveal nonspecific signal.
- Replicate samples and validated antibodies help separate biological variation from technical variation.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to antibody target](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_030.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to antibody target](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_031.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Western Blotting - Troubleshoot the blot


@left


- Weak signal can follow poor transfer, low target abundance, unsuitable antibodies, or lost antigen recognition.
- High background can follow insufficient blocking or washing, excess antibody, or nonspecific binding.
- Saturated bands are outside the useful measurement range; change exposure or sample amount and repeat.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to antibody target](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_031.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to antibody target](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_032.png)
=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


# 5. Isoelectric Points and Isoelectric Focusing


@top-left


![Instructor figure related to protein charge vs. ph](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_028.png)


@bottom-left


![Instructor figure related to protein charge vs. ph](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_017.jpg)


@right


- Protein charge vs. pH
- Isoelectric point
- Focused separation
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: Isoelectric Focusing


@left


- What is a protein's isoelectric point, and how does pH affect its net charge?
- How does isoelectric focusing separate proteins in a pH gradient?
- How does two-dimensional electrophoresis combine focusing with SDS-PAGE?


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to protein charge vs. ph](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_018.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to protein charge vs. ph](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_027.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Isoelectric Focusing


@left


- A protein's pI is the pH at which its average net charge is zero; above or below it, the net charge changes.
- In a stable pH gradient, a protein migrates until it reaches the position where the local pH equals its pI.
- Protein solubility is often lowest near the pI, but the effect depends on the protein and solution conditions.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to protein charge vs. ph](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_027.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to protein charge vs. ph](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_028.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Isoelectric Focusing - Two dimensions


@left


- Two-dimensional gels first separate proteins by pI, then by apparent molecular mass using SDS-PAGE.
- The resulting spots can reveal mixtures, isoforms, or some modification-dependent shifts.
- Theoretical pI estimates use sequence and ionizable groups; experimental values can differ because of modifications and conditions.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to protein charge vs. ph](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_028.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to protein charge vs. ph](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_017.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Isoelectric Points and Isoelectric Focusing - Protein charge changes with pH


@left


- Below its pI a protein tends to be net positive; above its pI it tends to be net negative.
- Near its pI, net charge is close to zero and some proteins become less soluble, depending on sequence and conditions.
- A theoretical pI estimate uses ionizable residues; modifications, local structure, and processing affect experimental behavior.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to protein charge vs. ph](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_017.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to protein charge vs. ph](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_018.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Isoelectric Points and Isoelectric Focusing - Separate by isoelectric point


@left


- Isoelectric focusing establishes a pH gradient in a gel or strip.
- A protein migrates until it reaches the region where pH equals its pI and net charge approaches zero.
- Charge-changing modifications can create multiple focused forms of the same protein.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to protein charge vs. ph](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_018.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to protein charge vs. ph](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_027.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Isoelectric Points and Isoelectric Focusing - Read a two-dimensional gel


@left


- The first dimension separates proteins by pI using isoelectric focusing.
- After equilibration with SDS, the second dimension separates focused proteins by apparent molecular mass.
- A spot pattern can reveal isoforms or modified forms, but spot matching and low-abundance detection need controls.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to protein charge vs. ph](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_027.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to protein charge vs. ph](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_028.png)
=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


# 6. Liquid Column Chromatography


@top-left


![Instructor figure related to mobile phase](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_035.jpg)


@bottom-left


![Instructor figure related to mobile phase](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_036.png)


@right


- Mobile phase
- Stationary phase
- Protein separation
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: Protein Chromatography


@left


- What are the mobile and stationary phases, and how do proteins interact with a column?
- How do size exclusion, ion exchange, hydrophobic interaction, reverse phase, and affinity chromatography differ?
- When are HPLC and FPLC useful for protein separation or purification?


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to mobile phase](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_036.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to mobile phase](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_037.gif)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Chromatography


@left


- The mobile phase carries the sample; the stationary phase selectively retains molecules according to a chosen property.
- Size exclusion separates by hydrodynamic size; ion exchange by charge; hydrophobic methods by surface interactions; affinity by specific binding.
- A sequence of orthogonal columns can remove different contaminants and improve purity.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to mobile phase](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_037.gif)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to mobile phase](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_038.jpg)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: Protein Chromatography - Choosing a method


@left


- Affinity columns capture a target through a specific ligand; salt, pH, or a competing ligand can release it.
- HIC uses salt-promoted hydrophobic interactions under comparatively mild conditions; reverse-phase conditions are often more denaturing.
- FPLC is commonly configured for biomolecule purification; HPLC covers a wider range of analytical and preparative separations.


@right


![Protein chromatography setup from the instructor ODP](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_035.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Liquid Column Chromatography - Four useful separation modes


@left

- Size exclusion: hydrodynamic size; larger proteins elute first
- Ion exchange: surface charge; salt or pH elution
- Hydrophobic interaction: high-salt binding
- Affinity: specific ligand capture

@top-right


![Instructor figure related to mobile phase](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_039.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to mobile phase](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_040.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Liquid Column Chromatography - Choose a protein-compatible system


@left


- Reverse-phase chromatography uses strongly hydrophobic phases and organic solvent that can disrupt protein structure.
- FPLC commonly uses aqueous buffers and protein-compatible resins; HPLC uses higher pressure and can support finer particles.
- Choice depends on protein stability, sample scale, purity target, buffer compatibility, and retained activity.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to mobile phase](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_040.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to mobile phase](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_035.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Liquid Column Chromatography - Build a purification workflow


@left


- Clarify the lysate, capture the target selectively, then use a second method with a different separation property.
- Track target and contaminants with an assay or gel at each step rather than relying on one final measurement.
- Finish with buffer exchange or size exclusion as needed, then measure concentration, purity, and function.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to mobile phase](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_035.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to mobile phase](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_036.png)
=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


# 7. Protein Tagging and Affinity Chromatography


@top-left


![Instructor figure related to affinity tags](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_039.jpg)


@bottom-left


![Instructor figure related to affinity tags](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_040.jpg)


@right


- Affinity tags
- Selective binding
- Protein purification
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: Protein Tags


@left


- How can an affinity tag make a recombinant protein easier to purify?
- Which ligands capture His-tag, FLAG-tag, or Strep-tag proteins?
- What should researchers check before deciding where to put or whether to remove a tag?


@top-right


![Student-created affinity-tag capture and purification workflow.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_affinity-tag-purification.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to affinity tags](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_039.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Tags


@left


- A DNA construct encodes the tag as part of the target protein, creating a fusion that can bind a matching ligand.
- His-tags commonly bind immobilized metal ions; FLAG and Strep tags use their corresponding specific binding partners.
- A typical column cycle loads the sample, washes away weakly bound material, then elutes the tagged protein.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to affinity tags](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_039.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to affinity tags](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_040.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Tags - Tradeoffs


@left


- Tags can simplify purification and detection, but may change folding, activity, solubility, or localization.
- N-terminal and C-terminal placement can have different effects; test the construct rather than assuming either is neutral.
- A protease site can allow tag removal; verify cleavage and final protein purity before use.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to affinity tags](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_040.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Student-created affinity-tag capture and purification workflow.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_affinity-tag-purification.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Tagging and Affinity Chromatography - Match a tag to its ligand


@left


- A His-tag binds metal-chelate resins such as nickel or cobalt; imidazole competes with the tag during elution.
- FLAG-tag and Strep-tag bind their respective ligands or antibodies and can be eluted using tag-specific conditions.
- A tag can improve capture, but it may change folding, localization, binding, or activity.


@top-right


![Student-created affinity-tag capture and purification workflow.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_affinity-tag-purification.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to affinity tags](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_039.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Tagging and Affinity Chromatography - Purify and release a tagged protein


@left


- Clone the tag in frame with the target coding sequence and express the fusion in the chosen host.
- Load clarified sample, wash away unbound proteins, and elute by changing pH, salt, or competing ligand.
- A protease site can allow tag removal; a second cleanup separates the released protein from tag and protease.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to affinity tags](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_039.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to affinity tags](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_040.jpg)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: Protein Tagging and Affinity Chromatography - Choose tag position and conditions


@left


- An N-terminal tag may disrupt a signal peptide; a C-terminal tag may interfere with a functional region.
- Test more than one tag position when function matters, comparing yield, solubility, purity, and activity.
- Specific capture can reduce steps, while tag removal and resin cost add time and expense.


@right


![Student-created affinity-tag capture and purification workflow.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_affinity-tag-purification.jpg)
=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


# 8. Mass Spectrometry


@top-left


![Instructor figure related to ionization](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_043.png)


@bottom-left


![Instructor figure related to ionization](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_044.png)


@right


- Ionization
- Mass-to-charge
- Protein identification
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: Protein Mass Spectrometry


@left


- What do the ion source, mass analyzer, and detector do in a mass spectrometer?
- How do MALDI and electrospray create ions, and how do analyzers separate them?
- How does tandem MS help identify or quantify proteins in complex samples?


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to ionization](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_043.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to ionization](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_044.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Mass Spectrometry


@left


- The ion source creates charged molecules; the analyzer separates ions by mass-to-charge ratio; the detector records their signals.
- MALDI and electrospray are soft-ionization approaches suited to biomolecules, with different sample and instrument workflows.
- Protease digestion makes peptides that can be separated, measured, and matched to sequence databases.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to ionization](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_044.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to ionization](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_045.png)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: Protein Mass Spectrometry - From spectra to proteins


@left


- Tandem MS fragments selected peptide ions; fragment patterns support sequence matching and protein identification.
- Quantification can compare ion signals, labeled standards, or multiplexed tags, depending on the method.
- Results depend on sample preparation, instrument coverage, database quality, and statistical filtering.


@right


![Mass spectrometer components from the instructor ODP](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_041.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Mass Spectrometry - Prepare a protein sample for MS


@left


- Complex mixtures are often reduced, alkylated, and digested with a protease such as trypsin.
- Peptide separation by liquid chromatography reduces simultaneous signals and helps resolve complex samples.
- Contaminants, incomplete digestion, and sample loss can distort detection and estimated abundance.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to ionization](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_046.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to ionization](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_047.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Mass Spectrometry - Ionize and separate peptides


@left


- Electrospray transfers peptides from solution into charged droplets and gas-phase ions.
- MALDI uses a matrix and laser pulse to create ions from a prepared sample spot.
- Time-of-flight, quadrupole, and Orbitrap analyzers use different principles to separate ions by mass-to-charge ratio.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to ionization](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_047.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to ionization](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_048.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Mass Spectrometry - Use tandem MS for identification


@left


- An instrument selects a peptide ion, fragments it, and measures fragment masses to produce an MS/MS spectrum.
- Fragment patterns are compared with theoretical peptides from sequence databases to infer peptide identity.
- Label-free signal, isotope labels, or targeted transitions can support relative or targeted quantification.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to ionization](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_048.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to ionization](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_049.png)
=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


# 9. Protein Sequencing


@top-left


![Student-created workflow from sample collection to protein identification.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_proteomics-workflow.jpg)


@bottom-left


![Instructor figure related to peptide fragments](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_041.png)


@right


- Peptide fragments
- Sequence evidence
- Protein identity
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: Protein Sequencing


@left


- How do protease digestion, Edman degradation, and tandem mass spectrometry contribute to sequence analysis?
- Why can peptide sequencing be harder than reading a DNA sequence?
- What can prevent complete sequence coverage or an unambiguous identification?


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to peptide fragments](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_042.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to peptide fragments](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_043.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Sequencing


@left


- Proteases cut proteins at characteristic residues, creating peptides that can be analyzed separately.
- Edman chemistry identifies an N-terminal residue through repeated cycles, but works best on short peptides with an accessible N-terminus.
- Tandem MS infers peptide sequence from fragment masses and compares the evidence with candidate sequences.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to peptide fragments](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_043.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to peptide fragments](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_044.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Sequencing - Limits


@left


- Blocked termini, disulfide bonds, modifications, and incomplete digestion can complicate sequence coverage.
- Leucine and isoleucine have the same nominal and exact mass, so mass alone does not distinguish them.
- Proteins cannot be amplified by PCR; low abundance must be handled through enrichment, sensitive instruments, or targeted assays.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to peptide fragments](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_044.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to peptide fragments](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_045.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Sequencing - Generate peptides for sequencing


@left


- Purify the protein or complex, then digest it into overlapping peptides with one or more proteases.
- Trypsin commonly cleaves after lysine or arginine, with exceptions based on the next residue and local context.
- Different cleavage specificities create overlapping evidence that helps assemble sequence coverage.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to peptide fragments](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_045.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to peptide fragments](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_046.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Sequencing - Compare Edman and MS methods


@left


- Edman degradation labels and removes the N-terminal residue in repeated cycles.
- Blocked N-termini, long sequences, or mixtures limit Edman analysis; disulfide bonds may need reduction first.
- Tandem mass spectrometry reads peptide fragments in parallel and can identify sequences by database matching.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to peptide fragments](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_046.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to peptide fragments](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_047.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Sequencing - Interpret sequence evidence


@left


- Protein is not amplified like DNA by PCR, so sensitivity depends on the amount and quality of sample.
- Leucine and isoleucine have the same mass, so ordinary MS/MS mass differences do not distinguish them reliably.
- Coverage gaps, chemical modifications, and unexpected cleavage require review or an independent method.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to peptide fragments](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_047.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Student-created workflow from sample collection to protein identification.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_proteomics-workflow.jpg)
=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


# 10. Phage Display


@top-left


![Instructor figure related to display library](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_048.png)


@bottom-left


![Instructor figure related to display library](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_049.png)


@right


- Display library
- Binding selection
- Candidate enrichment
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: Phage Display


@left


- How does phage display connect a peptide or protein to the DNA that encodes it?
- What are the main steps in library construction and selection for a binder?
- How can repeated selection support antibody discovery or other biotechnology applications?


@top-right


![Student-created phage display library and selection cycle.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_phage-display-selection.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to display library](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_048.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Phage Display


@left


- A library displays many peptide or protein variants on bacteriophage while retaining each variant's encoding DNA inside.
- The library is exposed to a target; unbound phage are washed away and retained phage are recovered and amplified.
- Repeating selection can enrich binders, but the result depends on library diversity, target presentation, and selection conditions.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to display library](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_048.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to display library](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_049.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Phage Display - Applications


@left


- Selected binders can be sequenced, tested for specificity, and developed as research or therapeutic candidates.
- Phage display has supported antibody discovery, epitope mapping, and studies of molecular recognition.
- Binding in a selection assay is a starting point; follow-up tests must check affinity, selectivity, and function.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to display library](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_049.png)


@bottom-right


![Student-created phage display library and selection cycle.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_phage-display-selection.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Phage Display - Link genotype to displayed protein


@left


- A peptide or protein coding sequence is fused to a phage coat-protein gene so the product appears on the phage surface.
- The phage carries the DNA encoding the displayed molecule, linking a binding phenotype to its genotype.
- A diverse sequence library can represent many candidate binders, subject to library size and folding constraints.


@top-right


![Student-created phage display library and selection cycle.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_phage-display-selection.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to display library](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_048.png)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: Phage Display - Select and enrich binders


@left


- Expose the library to an immobilized target, wash away weak or nonspecific binders, and recover retained phage.
- Amplify recovered phage in bacteria and repeat selection with tuned target concentration and washing conditions.
- Sequence enriched clones, then test purified candidates for binding strength, selectivity, and function.


@right


![Student-created phage display library and selection cycle.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_phage-display-selection.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Phage Display - Turn selection into a useful reagent


@left


- Counter-selection against related proteins can reduce cross-reactivity.
- Strong phage binding does not guarantee that a soluble antibody fragment behaves the same way.
- Follow-up tests assess affinity, specificity, stability, expression yield, and performance in the intended assay.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to display library](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_049.png)


@bottom-right


![Student-created phage display library and selection cycle.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_phage-display-selection.jpg)
=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


# 11. ELISA and Rapid Protein Tests


@top-left


![Instructor figure related to antigen capture](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_054.png)


@bottom-left


![Instructor figure related to antigen capture](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_055.png)


@right


- Antigen capture
- Enzyme-linked signal
- Rapid testing
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: ELISA and Rapid Tests


@left


- How do antigen-antibody binding and an enzyme-linked signal make an ELISA measurable?
- How do sandwich and indirect ELISA formats differ?
- How are plate ELISAs related to rapid lateral-flow tests, and what do their results mean?


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to antigen capture](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_057.png)


@bottom-right


![Student-created diagram of indirect ELISA binding and signal generation.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_indirect-elisa.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: ELISA


@left


- In a sandwich ELISA, a capture antibody retains the target and a second antibody supplies a detectable signal.
- In an indirect ELISA, sample antibodies bind an immobilized antigen and an enzyme-linked secondary antibody reveals binding.
- Standards, positive and negative controls, and a suitable detection range are needed to interpret signal quantitatively.


@top-right


![Student-created diagram of indirect ELISA binding and signal generation.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_indirect-elisa.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to antigen capture](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_050.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: ELISA - Diagnostics and rapid tests


@left


- ELISA can detect antigens or antibodies in medical, food-safety, and environmental testing.
- Rapid lateral-flow tests use labeled binding reagents moving along a strip; they are related immunoassays, not simply miniature plate ELISAs.
- A positive or negative result depends on assay sensitivity, specificity, sample timing, and the tested population.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to antigen capture](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_050.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to antigen capture](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_051.png)
=== layout: two-panels


# Answers: ELISA and Rapid Protein Tests - Choose an ELISA format


@left


- A sandwich ELISA captures antigen between two antibodies when compatible antibody pairs are available.
- An indirect ELISA detects sample antibodies bound to an immobilized antigen using an enzyme-linked secondary antibody.
- A competitive ELISA can suit small targets with limited antibody-binding sites.


@right


![Student-created diagram of indirect ELISA binding and signal generation.](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/student_indirect-elisa.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: ELISA and Rapid Protein Tests - Build a controlled assay


@left


- Select antibodies for target specificity, compatible binding sites, and performance in the chosen sample type.
- Include blanks, positive and negative controls, and a dilution series or standard curve when estimating concentration.
- Antibody affinity, sample matrix, incubation time, washing, and substrate reaction affect the measured signal.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to antigen capture](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_052.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to antigen capture](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_053.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: ELISA and Rapid Protein Tests - Interpret diagnostic signals


@left


- A lateral-flow test moves sample and labeled reagents along a membrane; a control line checks fluid and reagent function.
- Results depend on sensitivity, specificity, cutoff, sample timing, and prevalence in the tested population.
- Food and environmental tests can detect allergens, pathogens, toxins, or residues when validated for that sample matrix.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to antigen capture](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_053.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to antigen capture](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_054.png)
=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


# 12. Protein Quantification Methods


@top-left


![Instructor figure related to protein amount](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_019.png)


@bottom-left


![Instructor figure related to protein amount](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_020.jpg)


@right


- Protein amount
- Calibration
- Assay limits
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: Protein Quantification


@left


- How do Bradford, Lowry, and BCA assays estimate total protein in a sample?
- How can Western blotting or ELISA estimate one selected protein?
- When is mass spectrometry useful for measuring many proteins at once?


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to protein amount](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_020.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to protein amount](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_031.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Quantification


@left


- Colorimetric assays compare sample color with protein standards; reagent chemistry and protein composition affect the response.
- A standard curve helps convert signal to an estimate, within the assay's validated range.
- Western blotting and ELISA can quantify a target protein relative to standards or controls, but each is assay-dependent.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to protein amount](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_031.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to protein amount](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_032.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Quantification - Choosing a method


@left


- Use a total-protein assay for an overall sample estimate; use a target-specific assay to measure one protein.
- Mass spectrometry can profile many proteins in one experiment, but sample preparation and data analysis shape coverage.
- Choose based on sample complexity, sensitivity, throughput, available standards, and whether the question is total or target-specific.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to protein amount](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_032.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to protein amount](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_050.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Quantification Methods - Measure total protein


@left


- Bradford, Lowry, and BCA assays convert protein-dependent chemistry into a color signal measured against standards.
- Proteins can produce different responses because dye binding and reagent chemistry depend on amino-acid composition.
- Detergents, reducing agents, salts, and buffers can interfere, so standards and blanks should match sample conditions.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to protein amount](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_050.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to protein amount](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_052.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Quantification Methods - Choose a target-specific assay


@left


- Western blotting provides band position and relative signal for one target, with substantial sample handling and antibody dependence.
- ELISA measures a selected target in plate format and can support higher throughput after assay development.
- Use a validated standard curve within its linear range; dilute samples that exceed the calibrated signal range.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to protein amount](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_052.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to protein amount](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_019.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Quantification Methods - Quantify many proteins


@left


- Mass spectrometry can quantify many proteins in a mixture by measuring selected peptides or broader peptide signals.
- Stable-isotope standards can improve comparison by controlling for peptide recovery and instrument response.
- Report quantification strategy, normalization, missing-value handling, and uncertainty with the protein list.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to protein amount](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_019.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to protein amount](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_020.jpg)
=== layout: two-plus-one-panels


# 13. Protein-Protein Interaction Networks


@top-left


![Instructor figure related to molecular contacts](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_081.jpg)


@bottom-left


![Instructor figure related to molecular contacts](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_029.png)


@right


- Molecular contacts
- Interaction evidence
- Network models
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Questions: Protein Interaction Networks


@left


- How do yeast two-hybrid, co-immunoprecipitation, and mass spectrometry find possible protein interactions?
- How can interaction data be represented as a network and connected to pathways or disease?
- What evidence is needed before a predicted interaction becomes a useful biological conclusion?


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to molecular contacts](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_052.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to molecular contacts](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_057.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Interaction Networks


@left


- Yeast two-hybrid tests whether paired proteins can bring reporter components together in a cell.
- Co-immunoprecipitation isolates a protein complex; mass spectrometry can identify proteins recovered with it.
- Databases combine published evidence, but detected associations can depend on conditions and experimental design.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to molecular contacts](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_057.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to molecular contacts](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_081.jpg)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein Interaction Networks - Interpretation


@left


- A network edge represents evidence for an association; it does not always prove direct binding.
- Hubs and pathway neighborhoods can suggest follow-up experiments or candidate drug targets.
- Validate key edges in relevant cells and conditions; false positives, missing data, and biased sampling affect network maps.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to molecular contacts](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_081.jpg)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to molecular contacts](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_029.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein-Protein Interaction Networks - Measure protein interactions


@left


- Yeast two-hybrid tests whether two fused proteins bring transcriptional components together in a reporter system.
- Co-immunoprecipitation enriches a bait protein and associated partners from a cell extract under chosen buffer conditions.
- Protein arrays can test many binding events, but immobilization can change protein orientation or activity.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to molecular contacts](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_029.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to molecular contacts](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_052.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein-Protein Interaction Networks - Build an interaction network


@left


- Combine experimental interactions with curated databases and label edges by method, confidence, and evidence source.
- A network hub has many recorded partners, but high connectivity can reflect study attention as well as biology.
- Replicate experiments and orthogonal methods help distinguish direct binding from indirect co-complex association.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to molecular contacts](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_052.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to molecular contacts](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_057.png)
=== layout: one-plus-two-panels


# Answers: Protein-Protein Interaction Networks - Use networks to form hypotheses


@left


- Changed interaction partners can point to altered pathways or candidate disease mechanisms.
- Network analysis can nominate proteins or interfaces for follow-up, but a predicted hub is not automatically a drug target.
- Perturbation experiments test whether changing one interaction affects a cell behavior or disease-relevant phenotype.


@top-right


![Instructor figure related to molecular contacts](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_057.png)


@bottom-right


![Instructor figure related to molecular contacts](assets/lect04c-talking_points_set_3/image_081.jpg)
